Z||000003||rogergarrison||01-05-2001||01:43 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Old Lar:<BR><B>I had mail sent to many that seem to be from me.  I changed my log on password.  I got the same stuff back through someone elses e-mail site.  What was nasty was the e-mail was links to porno sites.  I got some real good hate mail after that.  I don't know how the hackers do that.  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I think somehow when u log on to some sites, they get your email addy, and they use it to send out other stuff like the porn. I think they do that because now sometimes mail wont go thru with fictisous names.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||DaRkLoRD||01-05-2001||02:07 PM||||I messed around with that when I was younger, about 1995.. back in the good old days when the net was new..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> nothing serious, just a few email pranks. I got caught though, when a couple of my friends told the people involved.. oh well, I guess I deserved it.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>it's pretty easy to do, unless the sysadmin actually checks the IP to see who's logged in. You can spoof email from pretty much anyone elses account on the same ISP as you, and it will seem legit until the sysadmin looks up the IP and sees that it wasn't your username. and if it was your account and you didn't send the emails, then someone had access to your password.. but.. it's not necessary to have their password to do basic email spoofing.<P>I wouldn't do anything like that now, but 5-6 years ago, I was pretty stupid. LOL<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||3
